# TypeScript conventions

Most of these rules are enforced by **ESLint** (`eslint.config.mjs`, strict
type-checked typescript-eslint presets plus project rules in `tools/eslint/`)
and **Prettier** (`.prettierrc.json`). `npm run check` must be green; never
disable a rule to get there – fix the code. The rare justified exception uses
`// eslint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <reason>` (e.g. `Mat4.set` with its 16
elements).

## Formatting (Prettier)

- **At most 80 characters per line** – code, comments and JSDoc (long string
  literals such as test titles are the only exception).
- 2 spaces, single quotes, semicolons, trailing commas, LF line endings.
- Run `npm run format` (or format on save); never hand-format against Prettier.

## Structure

- **One class per file**, file name = class name (`Mat4.ts`). A file with a
  class contains **only that class** (plus unexported constants); helper
  functions live in their own module (`helpers.ts`, `mat4Helpers.ts`,
  `clipping.ts`, …).
- **Types are separated from code:** every exported `interface` / `type`
  lives in the `types.ts` of its folder, and `types.ts` contains no runtime
  code. Types used by a single file may stay there, unexported.
- Type-only imports and exports use `import type` / `export type`.
- Every folder has an `index.ts` (barrel) that exports the public API of the
  folder. ES modules, named exports, **no** default exports.
- Imports and exports are sorted (`simple-import-sort`).

## Code blocks and control flow

- **Always braces** for `if`/`else`/`for`/`while`, also for one-liners
  (`curly: all`).
- `===` only; no nested ternaries; no `else` after `return`.
- Small units: complexity ≤ 12, nesting depth ≤ 3, ≤ 50 lines and ≤ 25
  statements per function, ≤ 4 parameters (use an options object), ≤ 300
  lines per file. Split instead of growing.
- No parameter reassignment, no implicit coercion (`+x`, `!!x`), no
  `console` in library code.

## Types

- `strict`, `noImplicitOverride`, `noImplicitReturns`,
  `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch` are on.
- No `any` (use `unknown` + a type guard). No `!` non-null assertion outside
  tests – check the case properly.
- Explicit return types on functions; `override` on members that implement or
  override a base class member; `readonly` where a member is never
  reassigned.
- Options objects instead of long parameter lists; options have sensible
  defaults (`interface FooOptions` in `types.ts` + defaults in the
  constructor).

## Documentation

- Code, identifiers, comments and JSDoc in **English**.
- JSDoc on every class, function, interface, type alias and **public
  method** (members marked `override` inherit their base class docs). State
  units (radians, pixels) and whether the object is mutated.
- Comments explain *why*; the code says *what*.

## Other

- No side effects on import (the package is `sideEffects: false`).
- Numeric types and math follow `math.mdc`.
